runs.text:
Have clients send an `Idempotency-Key` header, store it on the order, and make it unique per account. Scoping the key to the account means two accounts can't collide, and one account can't fetch another's order by reusing its key.

```sql
ALTER TABLE orders ADD COLUMN idempotency_key text;
CREATE UNIQUE INDEX orders_account_idempotency_key ON orders (account_id, idempotency_key);
```

In the route, insert with `ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING`. When the key already exists, `db.write` gets no `RETURNING` row back and returns `undefined`. The route then looks up the existing order and replays it:

```js
router.post("/", async (req, res) => {
  const key = req.get("Idempotency-Key");
  const row = await db.write(
    "INSERT INTO orders (account_id, sku, quantity, idempotency_key) VALUES ($1, $2, $3, $4) ON CONFLICT (account_id, idempotency_key) DO NOTHING RETURNING *",
    [req.user.accountId, req.body.sku, req.body.quantity, key]
  );
  if (row) return res.status(201).json(row);

  const [existing] = await db.query(
    "SELECT * FROM orders WHERE account_id = $1 AND idempotency_key = $2",
    [req.user.accountId, key]
  );
  if (existing.sku !== req.body.sku || Number(existing.quantity) !== Number(req.body.quantity)) {
    return res.status(422).json({ error: "Idempotency-Key reused with a different request" });
  }
  res.status(201).json(existing);
});
```

The lookup runs as its own statement after the insert for a reason. When two requests with the same key arrive at once, the second insert waits for the first to commit, and only a statement that starts after that commit can see the first order.

Should the header be required? Postgres treats NULLs as distinct in a unique index, so requests without a key still create an order every time, unprotected. Requiring the header closes that gap but breaks any client that doesn't send one yet. Can all your clients send it?

turns.text:
Scope each write in its `WHERE` clause with `req.user.accountId`, which `auth.js:11` already sets. When no row comes back, return 404. `db.write` returns `res.rows[0]`, so an update that matches nothing currently yields `undefined`, and the handler replies 200 with an empty body. Use 404 rather than 403 so the response doesn't reveal that another account's id exists.

**Orders** (`routes/orders.js:14`):

```js
router.patch("/:id/cancel", async (req, res) => {
  const row = await db.write(
    "UPDATE orders SET status = 'cancelled' WHERE id = $1 AND account_id = $2 RETURNING *",
    [req.params.id, req.user.accountId]
  );
  if (!row) return res.status(404).end();
  res.json(row);
});
```

**Accounts** (`routes/accounts.js:6` and `:14`): each user belongs to one account, so `:id` is only valid when it equals the caller's account. Put that check in the SQL so Postgres handles the type comparison. Comparing the string in `req.params.id` with `accountId` in JavaScript can fail depending on the column type.

```js
router.patch("/:id", async (req, res) => {
  const row = await db.write(
    "UPDATE accounts SET display_name = $1, billing_email = $2 WHERE id = $3 AND id = $4 RETURNING *",
    [req.body.display_name, req.body.billing_email, req.params.id, req.user.accountId]
  );
  if (!row) return res.status(404).end();
  res.json(row);
});

router.delete("/:id", async (req, res) => {
  const row = await db.write(
    "UPDATE accounts SET deleted_at = now() WHERE id = $1 AND id = $2 RETURNING id",
    [req.params.id, req.user.accountId]
  );
  if (!row) return res.status(404).end();
  res.status(204).end();
});
```

One decision can't be settled from the code: this stops writes across accounts, but any member of an account can still rename it or soft-delete it, because the session carries no role. Should account edits require an admin role?

This bug is separate, but you'll hit it before you can test the fix: `app.js` never mounts `cookie-parser`, so `req.cookies` is undefined and `sessionUser` returns 401 on every request.

